    <h1 class="display reveal" data-d="1">What drives the cost of a custom AI tool?</h1>
    <p class="lead reveal" data-d="2">Less the AI itself than everything around it: how clear the problem is, the state of your data, the systems it connects to and how accurate it must be.</p>
  </div>
</header>

<main id="main" tabindex="-1">
<!-- ARTICLE -->
<section class="section surface-page" id="article" style="padding-top:clamp(40px,6vw,80px)">
  <div class="wrap">
    <article class="post">
      <p class="post-byline">By <a href="../About">Taz Abbas</a>, Managing Partner<span aria-hidden="true"> · </span><span>Published <time datetime="2026-10-29">October 29, 2026</time></span><span aria-hidden="true"> · </span><a href="../ai-solutions">AI Solutions Development</a></p>
      <div class="prose">
<h2 id="short-answer">The short answer</h2>
<p>The cost of a custom AI tool depends less on the AI itself than on everything around it: how clear the problem is, how messy the data is, how many systems it must connect to, how accurate it has to be, and who will look after it once it is live. The AI model is often a modest share of the total. A clear, narrow problem with clean data and one or two integrations costs far less than a broad one with scattered data and strict accuracy needs.</p>

<h2 id="the-cost-drivers">The main cost drivers</h2>
<p>When you ask for a price, these are the factors that move it most.</p>

<h3 id="scope">1. How well the problem is defined</h3>
<p>"Use AI to improve customer service" could mean anything. "Read incoming support emails, tag them by product and urgency, and draft a first reply for an agent to approve" can be scoped, built and tested. The clearer the task, the less time is spent discovering what the tool should do, and the fewer expensive changes come later. Paying for a short discovery phase to define the problem is usually the cheapest money in the project.</p>

<h3 id="data">2. The state of your data</h3>
<p>AI tools work on your documents, records and messages. If those are in one system, in consistent formats, with the fields filled in, the build is straightforward. If they are spread across inboxes, shared drives and spreadsheets, with duplicates and gaps, a significant share of the effort goes into finding, cleaning and organizing them. This work is unglamorous and often underestimated.</p>

<h3 id="integrations">3. The systems it must connect to</h3>
<p>Each system the tool reads from or writes to adds work: understanding its API, handling sign-in and permissions, mapping fields, and dealing with the cases where it behaves unexpectedly. One integration with a modern system that has a well-documented API is simple. Several, or an older system with no API, can dominate the budget.</p>

<h3 id="accuracy">4. How accurate it needs to be</h3>
<p>A tool that drafts suggestions for a person to review can tolerate occasional mistakes. A tool that acts on its own, or whose errors reach customers or affect money, needs far more testing, checks and safeguards. Moving from "useful most of the time" to "reliable enough to act without review" is often where costs rise sharply. Keeping a person in the loop is not only safer, it is usually much cheaper.</p>

<h3 id="interface">5. Who uses it and how</h3>
<p>A tool that runs in the background and drops results into a system your team already uses needs little interface. A tool with its own screens, user accounts, roles and reporting needs design and front-end work. Ask whether people really need a new application or whether the output could arrive where they already work.</p>

<h3 id="security">6. Security, privacy and compliance</h3>
<p>Customer data, health information, financial records and anything covered by regulation bring requirements for where data is stored, who can see it, how long it is kept and how actions are logged. Meeting these is essential and adds design, build and review time. Raise them at the start, not after the tool is built.</p>

<h3 id="testing">7. How it will be tested</h3>
<p>Testing an AI tool means running it against a set of real examples with known correct answers, measuring how often it gets them right, and repeating that check whenever something changes. Building that test set takes time from people who know the work, and it is one of the most valuable parts of the project.</p>

<h2 id="running-costs">The running costs</h2>
<p>A custom AI tool has ongoing costs as well as the build:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Usage fees.</strong> Most AI models are paid for by usage, so cost grows with the volume of documents or requests processed. Estimate volume early.</li>
  <li><strong>Hosting.</strong> The tool has to run somewhere, along with any databases and storage it uses.</li>
  <li><strong>Maintenance.</strong> Connected systems change, models are updated or retired, and new edge cases appear. Budget for regular upkeep.</li>
  <li><strong>Improvement.</strong> Once people use the tool, they will ask for more. Decide how those requests are handled and funded.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="keeping-cost-down">How to keep the cost down</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Start with one narrow task and prove it works before expanding.</li>
  <li>Use the systems you already have as the place where results land.</li>
  <li>Keep a person reviewing outputs until accuracy has been measured on real cases.</li>
  <li>Clean up the data for the first task only, not the whole business.</li>
  <li>Check whether a product you already pay for has added a feature that does the job.</li>
  <li>Ask for a fixed price for a defined first stage, with later stages priced once the first has taught you what you need.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="how-to-compare-quotes">How to compare quotes</h2>
<p>Quotes for the same idea can vary widely because each supplier has assumed a different scope. When you compare them, check what each includes: discovery, data preparation, integrations, testing on your real data, security work, training, handover, and support after launch. Ask what happens if accuracy falls short, and who owns the code and the data afterwards. A low quote that leaves out testing and upkeep is not cheaper. It moves the cost to later.</p>

<h2 id="worth-it">Is it worth it?</h2>
<p>Compare the full cost, build plus running, against the time and errors the tool removes and what your team can do with that time instead. If you cannot describe that benefit clearly, the problem is not yet defined well enough to build. That is the right moment to stop and define it, before spending on the build.</p>

      </div>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>

